<?php
/** Canonical 24-field content for geometric figures, volume/area, and probability/statistics. */
if (!defined('ABSPATH')) exit;

$build = static function ($title, $focus, $question, $vocabulary, $example, $mistake, $application, $practice, $challenge, $standards) {
    return [
        'title' => $title,
        'subtitle' => $focus,
        'essential_question' => $question,
        'learning_targets' => "I can represent the key ideas in {$title}.\nI can select and apply an appropriate strategy.\nI can explain my reasoning and check whether my result is reasonable.",
        'vocabulary' => $vocabulary,
        'worked_examples' => $example,
        'common_mistakes' => $mistake,
        'real_life' => $application,
        'videos' => $title . ' lesson and examples | https://www.khanacademy.org/math',
        'video_chapters' => "0:00 | Connect to prior knowledge\n1:15 | Define the core idea\n2:40 | Model a worked example\n4:20 | Analyze a common mistake\n5:45 | Try and verify",
        'practice_warmup' => $practice,
        'guided_practice' => $practice . "\nExplain which definition, formula, or representation supports the solution. | Show labeled work | Connect every step to the given information. | Check units and reasonableness.",
        'independent_practice' => $practice . "\nCreate and solve a second example with different values. | Answers vary | Use the same mathematical structure. | Verify with another method or representation.",
        'challenge_practice' => $challenge,
        'master_it' => "I can identify the important information.\nI can use accurate notation, labels, and units.\nI can justify and verify my conclusion.",
        'mastery_questions' => "What should happen before calculating? | Identify and label the given information ; Guess ; Ignore units ; Round every value | A\nWhich response gives the strongest evidence? | An answer only ; A labeled model with reasoning ; A copied rule ; An estimate only | B\nWhat is an effective accuracy check? | Compare the result with the context and definition ; Hide the steps ; Change the question ; Remove labels | A\nWhy are units or labels important? | They communicate what a value represents ; They make every answer larger ; They replace reasoning ; They are optional | A\nWhat should a student do after finding an error? | Locate the step, revise it, and recheck ; Erase all work ; Keep the answer ; Choose a random value | A",
        'parent_summary' => "In {$title}, students connect a visual or contextual model to precise mathematical reasoning. Encourage them to label information, explain why a strategy applies, and check the result.",
        'parent_conversation' => "Ask: What information is given and what must be found?\nAsk: Why does this strategy fit?\nAsk your child to show a visual, numerical, or verbal check.",
        'parent_five_minute' => "Choose a simple everyday example related to " . strtolower($title) . ". Have your child label the known information, solve or interpret it, and explain one accuracy check aloud.",
        'teacher_objectives' => "Students will model and solve problems involving " . strtolower($title) . ".\nStudents will communicate reasoning with precise vocabulary and representations.\nStudents will analyze errors and verify conclusions.",
        'teacher_pacing' => "Launch | 5 minutes | Use a notice-and-wonder context.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Connect vocabulary, models, and procedures.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Pause for predictions.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Move from guided to independent work.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Require reasoning and a check.",
        'teacher_misconceptions' => $mistake,
        'teacher_differentiation' => "Below Level | Use manipulatives, color-coded models, and partially completed examples.\nOn Level | Mix representations and require written justification.\nAdvanced | Add reverse reasoning, multiple constraints, or proof.\nMultilingual Learners | Pair vocabulary with diagrams, gestures, symbols, and sentence frames.",
        'teacher_formative' => "Listen for precise vocabulary during explanations.\nUse one error-analysis prompt during guided practice.\nCollect an exit ticket requiring a result, supporting work, and a check.",
        'standards' => $standards,
    ];
};

return [
    'geometric-figures-and-relationships' => $build('Geometric Figures and Relationships', 'Classify figures and use definitions, attributes, and relationships to justify conclusions.', 'How do defining attributes reveal relationships among geometric figures?', "Point, line, segment, ray, and angle — basic geometric objects\nParallel — coplanar lines that never intersect\nPerpendicular — lines meeting at 90 degrees\nPolygon — closed plane figure formed by segments\nHierarchy — classification in which a figure may belong to more than one group", "Classify a square | It has four equal sides, four right angles, opposite sides parallel. | It is a square, rectangle, rhombus, parallelogram, and quadrilateral.\nCompare vertical and adjacent angles | Vertical angles are congruent; adjacent angles share a side. | Use the relationship that matches the diagram.", "Classifying only by appearance | Mark defining attributes before naming the figure.\nTreating categories as mutually exclusive | Use the hierarchy: every square is also a rectangle and rhombus.\nAssuming all intersecting lines are perpendicular | Verify a right angle.", 'Geometric relationships support architecture, art, construction, maps, product design, and computer graphics.', 'A quadrilateral has four right angles and opposite sides equal. Classify it. | Rectangle | Four right angles guarantee a rectangle; it may be a square only if all sides are equal. | Mark the attributes.', 'Create a classification tree for quadrilaterals. Place one example and one nonexample in every category and justify each decision.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.5.G.B.3–4 — Classify two-dimensional figures in a hierarchy.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.A.2 and 8.G.A.5 — Draw figures and use angle relationships.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP3 and MP7 — Construct arguments and use structure."),

    'area-and-perimeter' => $build('Area and Perimeter', 'Distinguish boundary length from covered space and solve problems with standard formulas.', 'How do dimensions determine a figure’s perimeter and area?', "Perimeter — distance around a figure\nArea — number of square units covering a region\nBase and height — perpendicular dimensions used in area formulas\nSquare unit — unit used to measure area", "Rectangle 8 cm by 5 cm | P=2(8+5)=26 cm. | A=8×5=40 cm².\nTriangle with base 10 m and height 7 m | A=1/2(10)(7)=35 m².", "Using square units for perimeter | Perimeter uses linear units.\nAdding dimensions for area | Area measures covering and uses multiplication or decomposition.", 'Flooring, fencing, painting, landscaping, and packaging require area or perimeter.', 'Find the perimeter and area of a 9-by-4 rectangle. | 26 units; 36 square units | Apply P=2l+2w and A=lw. | Check units.', 'Design two rectangles with area 72 square units but different perimeters. Explain why equal area does not require equal perimeter.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.G.A.1 — Find areas of polygons.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.B.6 — Solve area and perimeter problems.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP4 and MP6 — Model and attend to precision."),
    'area-of-polygons' => $build('Area of Polygons', 'Decompose triangles, quadrilaterals, and regular polygons into familiar shapes.', 'How can decomposition produce reliable area formulas for polygons?', "Polygon — closed figure formed by segments\nDecompose — split into simpler figures\nParallelogram — quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides\nTrapezoid — quadrilateral with at least one pair of parallel sides\nApothem — perpendicular distance from a regular polygon’s center to a side", "Parallelogram b=12,h=5 | A=bh=60 square units.\nTrapezoid b₁=6,b₂=10,h=4 | A=1/2(6+10)(4)=32 square units.", "Using a slanted side as height | Height must be perpendicular to the base.\nForgetting the one-half factor for triangles or trapezoids | Connect the formula to decomposition.", 'Polygon area is used in land measurement, tiling, architecture, signs, and fabric design.', 'Find the area of a parallelogram with base 14 and height 6. | 84 square units | Multiply perpendicular base and height. | 14×6.', 'Decompose an irregular polygon in two different ways. Prove that both methods give the same total area.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.G.A.1 — Find area by composing and decomposing.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.B.6 — Solve problems involving two-dimensional figures.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP4 and MP7 — Model and use structure."),
    'circles' => $build('Circles', 'Connect radius, diameter, circumference, and area using pi.', 'How does the radius determine every important measurement of a circle?', "Center — point equidistant from the circle\nRadius — segment from center to circle\nDiameter — chord through the center equal to twice the radius\nCircumference — distance around a circle\nPi — ratio of circumference to diameter", "Circle with r=6 | C=2πr=12π≈37.7 units. | A=πr²=36π≈113.1 square units.\nDiameter 18 | r=9. | A=81π square units.", "Using diameter as radius | Divide diameter by 2 first.\nForgetting to square radius in area | Circumference is linear; area uses r².", 'Circles appear in wheels, gears, pipes, round tables, tracks, and circular gardens.', 'A circle has diameter 10. Find circumference and area. | 10π units; 25π square units | Radius is 5. | Use exact values.', 'A circular path surrounds a circular garden. Determine the path’s area from two radii and explain why subtracting areas works.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.B.4 — Use formulas for circumference and area of circles.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2, MP4, and MP6 — Reason, model, and attend to precision."),
    'composite-figures' => $build('Composite Figures', 'Find area by decomposing or subtracting familiar shapes.', 'How can a complex region be reorganized into simpler measurable parts?', "Composite figure — figure made from simpler shapes\nDecomposition — splitting a figure into nonoverlapping parts\nMissing dimension — length inferred from other labeled dimensions\nNet area — combined area after addition or subtraction", "L-shaped region | Enclose it in a 10×8 rectangle and subtract a 4×3 cutout. | A=80−12=68 square units.\nRectangle plus semicircle | Add rectangle area and half a circle’s area.", "Double-counting overlapping regions | Mark each nonoverlapping part.\nSubtracting an unlabeled length incorrectly | Use aligned total lengths to infer it.", 'Composite area supports floor plans, parks, logos, gardens, and material estimates.', 'A 12×9 rectangle has a 4×3 corner removed. Find area. | 96 square units | 108−12. | Check by decomposing.', 'Design a composite figure with total area 150 square units. Provide two valid solution methods and compare their efficiency.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.G.A.1 — Find area by composing and decomposing.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.B.6 — Solve real-world area problems.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP1 and MP4 — Persevere and model."),
    'surface-area' => $build('Surface Area', 'Use nets and face areas to measure the exterior of three-dimensional figures.', 'How does a two-dimensional net reveal the surface area of a solid?', "Face — flat surface of a solid\nNet — two-dimensional pattern that folds into a solid\nLateral area — area of side faces excluding bases\nSurface area — total area of all exterior faces", "Rectangular prism 3×4×5 | SA=2(3×4)+2(3×5)+2(4×5)=94 square units.\nTriangular prism | Add two triangular bases and three rectangular lateral faces.", "Finding volume instead of surface area | Surface area covers faces and uses square units.\nOmitting a face | Label every region on the net and pair congruent faces.", 'Surface area determines wrapping, paint, labels, insulation, and sheet material.', 'Find the surface area of a 2×3×6 rectangular prism. | 72 square units | 2(6)+2(12)+2(18). | Include all six faces.', 'Create two rectangular prisms with equal volume but different surface areas. Determine which uses less packaging and explain why.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.G.A.4 — Represent prisms with nets and find surface area.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.B.6 — Solve surface-area problems.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP4 and MP5 — Model and use tools."),
    'volume-of-prisms-and-cylinders' => $build('Volume of Prisms and Cylinders', 'Use base area and height to measure space inside prisms and cylinders.', 'Why does multiplying base area by height determine volume?', "Volume — space inside a solid\nBase area — area of the repeated cross section\nPrism — solid with congruent parallel bases\nCylinder — solid with congruent circular bases\nCubic unit — unit used for volume", "Rectangular prism 4×5×9 | V=lwh=180 cubic units.\nCylinder r=3,h=8 | V=πr²h=72π cubic units.", "Using diameter as radius | Convert before substituting.\nWriting square units for volume | Volume is measured in cubic units.", 'Volume is used for storage, tanks, rooms, shipping boxes, and liquid capacity.', 'Find volume of a prism with base area 24 and height 7. | 168 cubic units | V=Bh. | Multiply layers.', 'Two cylinders have equal volume. One has twice the radius of the other. Determine the required relationship between their heights.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.G.A.2 — Find volumes of right rectangular prisms.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.8.G.C.9 — Use cylinder volume formulas.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2 and MP4 — Reason and model."),
    'volume-of-pyramids-cones-and-spheres' => $build('Volume of Pyramids, Cones, and Spheres', 'Compare volumes of pointed and curved solids with related prisms and cylinders.', 'How do one-third relationships connect pyramids and cones to prisms and cylinders?', "Pyramid — solid with a polygon base and triangular faces meeting at a vertex\nCone — solid with a circular base and one vertex\nSphere — points in space equidistant from a center\nVolume — space inside a solid", "Cone r=4,h=9 | V=1/3π(4²)(9)=48π cubic units.\nSphere r=3 | V=4/3π(27)=36π cubic units.", "Omitting the one-third factor for cones or pyramids | Compare with a matching cylinder or prism.\nUsing slant height in a volume formula | Volume uses perpendicular height.", 'These formulas support tank design, architecture, food containers, astronomy, and manufacturing.', 'Find a pyramid’s volume when B=30 and h=12. | 120 cubic units | V=1/3Bh. | 30×12÷3.', 'Compare a cone, cylinder, and sphere that share appropriate dimensions. Determine and explain their exact volume ratios.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.B.6 — Solve volume problems.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.8.G.C.9 — Use cone, cylinder, and sphere formulas.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP4 and MP7 — Model and use structure."),
    'scale-drawings-and-dimensional-measurement' => $build('Scale Drawings and Dimensional Measurement', 'Use scale factors and unit reasoning to connect drawings, area, surface area, and volume.', 'How does a scale factor affect one-, two-, and three-dimensional measurements?', "Scale drawing — proportional representation\nScale factor — multiplier connecting corresponding lengths\nDimension — measurable direction\nConversion factor — ratio equal to one used to change units", "Scale factor 3 | Lengths multiply by 3, areas by 3²=9, and volumes by 3³=27.\nMap scale 1 cm:5 km and distance 7.2 cm | Actual distance=36 km.", "Applying a linear scale factor directly to area or volume | Square it for area and cube it for volume.\nMixing units inside a formula | Convert first.", 'Scale reasoning is used in maps, models, blueprints, prototypes, and unit conversions.', 'A model uses scale 1:4. An area is 6 cm². Find actual area. | 96 cm² | Area factor is 4²=16. | 6×16.', 'Redesign a package at 150% linear scale. Predict and justify the changes in surface area, volume, and material efficiency.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.A.1 — Solve scale-drawing problems.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.RP.A.2 and 8.G.A.4 — Use proportional and similarity reasoning.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2 and MP4 — Reason and model."),
    'cross-sections-and-solids-of-revolution' => $build('Cross-Sections and Solids of Revolution', 'Visualize the two-dimensional figures formed by slicing solids and the three-dimensional solids formed by rotating plane figures.', 'How do slicing and rotation connect two-dimensional figures with three-dimensional solids?', "Cross section — two-dimensional figure formed when a plane intersects a solid\nPlane — flat surface extending without end\nAxis of rotation — line around which a figure turns\nSolid of revolution — three-dimensional solid formed by rotating a plane figure\nPerpendicular — meeting at a right angle", "Slice a right circular cylinder parallel to its bases | The cross section is a circle congruent to the base.\nRotate a rectangle around one side | The rectangle sweeps out a cylinder whose height is the side on the axis and whose radius is the perpendicular side.", "Naming a cross section from the solid alone | The result also depends on the slice's direction and location.\nUsing the side on the axis as the radius | Radius is the perpendicular distance from the axis.\nConfusing a cross section with a net | A cross section comes from a slice; a net unfolds faces.", 'Medical scans, engineering drawings, manufacturing, architecture, and computer modeling use cross sections and solids of revolution.', 'A plane slices a rectangular prism parallel to its base. What shape is formed? | A rectangle congruent to the base | Parallel slices preserve the base shape and dimensions in a right prism. | Sketch the plane and label corresponding edges.', 'A right triangle with legs 5 and 12 rotates around the 12-unit leg. Identify the solid and determine its exact volume. Then explain how the dimensions of the triangle become dimensions of the solid.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.G.A.3 — Describe two-dimensional figures resulting from slicing three-dimensional figures.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.HSG.GMD.B.4 — Identify shapes of two-dimensional cross sections and three-dimensional objects generated by rotations.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP4 and MP7 — Model and use structure."),

    'statistical-questions-and-data-displays' => $build('Statistical Questions and Data Displays', 'Recognize variability and organize data in appropriate tables, plots, and graphs.', 'How does a statistical question guide data collection and display?', "Statistical question — question anticipating variability\nData set — collected values\nDot plot — number-line display using marks for frequency\nHistogram — display grouping numerical values into intervals\nBox plot — display showing a five-number summary", "Question: How many minutes do students read nightly? | Statistical because answers vary.\nData 2,3,3,5,7 | Dot plot places one dot at 2, two at 3, one at 5, one at 7.", "Calling every numerical question statistical | A statistical question anticipates multiple varying answers.\nUsing unequal histogram intervals | Keep bin widths consistent unless clearly justified.", 'Data displays communicate surveys, school results, health trends, weather, and business information.', 'Is “How tall am I?” statistical? | No | It seeks one fixed value. | Revise it to ask about a group.', 'Collect a small real data set and represent it with two different displays. Explain which display communicates the pattern better.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.SP.A.1–2 and B.4 — Recognize statistical questions and display distributions.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2 and MP4 — Reason and model."),
    'measures-of-center-and-variability' => $build('Measures of Center and Variability', 'Describe a distribution with center, spread, shape, and context.', 'How do center and variability work together to describe a data set?', "Mean — equal-share average\nMedian — middle ordered value\nMode — most frequent value\nRange — maximum minus minimum\nInterquartile range — Q3 minus Q1\nMean absolute deviation — average distance from the mean", "Data 3,5,5,7,10 | Mean=6; median=5; mode=5; range=7.\nIf Q1=12 and Q3=20 | IQR=8.", "Finding median before ordering | Sort first.\nReporting only the mean | Include spread and consider outliers and shape.", 'Center and variability summarize test results, prices, sports performance, and scientific measurements.', 'Find mean and median of 2,4,6,8. | Both 5 | Mean=20÷4; median=(4+6)÷2. | Compare.', 'Create two data sets with the same mean but very different variability. Explain why the mean alone is insufficient.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.SP.B.5 and 7.SP.B.4 — Summarize and compare distributions.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2, MP3, and MP6 — Reason, justify, and attend to precision."),
    'populations-and-samples' => $build('Populations and Samples', 'Use representative random samples to make careful inferences about populations.', 'When does a sample support a trustworthy conclusion about a population?', "Population — entire group of interest\nSample — subset studied\nRandom sample — selection method giving members a fair chance\nBias — systematic influence favoring some outcomes\nInference — conclusion about a population based on sample data", "Survey school lunch preference | Sampling only cafeteria workers is biased. | Randomly select students across grades.\n18 of 30 sampled students prefer option A | Estimate 60% of a similar population, with uncertainty.", "Using a convenience sample | Identify who is excluded.\nTreating an estimate as exact | Report it as an inference and discuss uncertainty.", 'Sampling informs elections, product research, public health, ecology, and school decisions.', 'Which is less biased: first 20 arrivals or 20 randomly selected names? | Random names | Arrival time may relate to the response. | Compare coverage.', 'Design a sampling plan for a schoolwide question. Identify two possible biases and explain how the design reduces them.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.SP.A.1–2 — Use random samples to draw inferences.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP3 and MP4 — Construct arguments and model."),
    'theoretical-and-experimental-probability' => $build('Theoretical and Experimental Probability', 'Compare predicted likelihood with observed long-run relative frequency.', 'How do models and experiments work together to describe chance?', "Probability — number from 0 to 1 describing likelihood\nTheoretical probability — favorable equally likely outcomes divided by all outcomes\nExperimental probability — observed successes divided by trials\nSample space — set of all possible outcomes", "Fair die: P(even)=3/6=1/2.\nSpinner lands blue 27 times in 60 trials | Experimental P(blue)=27/60=0.45.", "Expecting short experiments to match theory exactly | Relative frequency stabilizes over many trials.\nCounting outcomes as equally likely without justification | Examine the model.", 'Probability supports games, insurance, weather forecasts, medicine, and risk decisions.', 'A bag has 3 red and 5 blue counters. Find P(red). | 3/8 | Three favorable of eight total. | Check between 0 and 1.', 'Design and run or simulate an experiment whose theoretical probability is 3/5. Compare results across increasing trial counts.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.SP.C.5–7 — Understand and approximate probability.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2, MP4, and MP5 — Reason, model, and use tools."),
    'compound-probability' => $build('Compound Probability', 'Use organized sample spaces and multiplication or addition rules for multistep events.', 'How can a sample space reveal probabilities of compound events?', "Compound event — event with two or more outcomes or stages\nIndependent events — one outcome does not affect another\nDependent events — one outcome changes later probabilities\nTree diagram — branching display of sequential outcomes", "Flip a coin and roll a die | 12 equally likely ordered outcomes. | P(heads and even)=3/12=1/4.\nDraw two red counters without replacement | The second probability changes, so events are dependent.", "Adding probabilities for an “and” event | For independent stages, multiply.\nReplacing after a draw without stating it | Replacement determines independence.", 'Compound probability informs reliability, genetics, games, security, and risk analysis.', 'Flip two fair coins. Find P(two heads). | 1/4 | HH is one of HH, HT, TH, TT. | Use a list or tree.', 'Build a multistage game with a target probability between 0.20 and 0.30. Prove the exact probability and evaluate fairness.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.SP.C.8 — Find probabilities of compound events.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP1, MP4, and MP7 — Persevere, model, and use structure."),
    'scatter-plots-and-association' => $build('Scatter Plots and Association', 'Analyze patterns between two numerical variables and use trend lines cautiously.', 'What can a scatter plot show—and not show—about two variables?', "Bivariate data — paired values for two variables\nScatter plot — graph of ordered data pairs\nAssociation — pattern relating variables\nOutlier — point far from the overall pattern\nLine of fit — line summarizing a roughly linear trend", "Study time and score show an upward pattern | Positive association.\nLine of fit y=5x+60 | At x=4, predict 80. | Treat as an estimate.", "Claiming association proves causation | Consider lurking variables and study design.\nExtrapolating far beyond the data | Limit predictions to a reasonable domain.", 'Scatter plots are used in science, education, economics, sports, and health research.', 'A plot falls from left to right. Describe association. | Negative | As one variable increases, the other tends to decrease. | Note strength separately.', 'Create a plausible data set with a strong association and one outlier. Compare a fit with and without the outlier.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.8.SP.A.1–3 — Construct and interpret scatter plots and linear models.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2 and MP4 — Reason and model."),
    'two-way-tables-and-categorical-data' => $build('Two-Way Tables and Categorical Data', 'Organize paired categorical data and compare joint, marginal, and conditional frequencies.', 'How do conditional percentages reveal associations in categorical data?', "Two-way table — table classifying data by two variables\nJoint frequency — count in an interior cell\nMarginal frequency — row or column total\nConditional relative frequency — proportion within a selected row or column", "40 students: 18 play a sport; 12 of those prefer morning classes | Conditional percent=12/18≈66.7%.\nCompare within-group percentages rather than raw counts when group sizes differ.", "Dividing by the grand total for a conditional question | Use the stated group’s row or column total.\nComparing counts from unequal groups | Compare relative frequencies.", 'Two-way tables analyze surveys, medical studies, marketing, and school-program participation.', 'In a row total of 25, 15 answer yes. Find conditional percent. | 60% | 15÷25. | Identify the condition first.', 'Construct two tables with identical marginal totals but different associations. Explain how conditional percentages expose the difference.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.8.SP.A.4 — Analyze patterns in two-way tables.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2, MP3, and MP6 — Reason, justify, and attend to precision."),
    'comparing-data-distributions' => $build('Comparing Data Distributions', 'Compare groups using visual overlap, center, variability, and context.', 'How can differences in distributions support a fair comparison between groups?', "Distribution — pattern of values and frequencies\nShape — symmetry, skew, peaks, and gaps\nOverlap — shared region of two distributions\nVariability — amount of spread\nInformal inference — evidence-based comparison without a formal test", "Group A median 72,IQR 8; Group B median 80,IQR 9 | B has higher center with similar spread.\nDifference in means is 6 while MADs are about 2 | Difference is large relative to variability.", "Comparing only maximum values | Use typical values and spread.\nIgnoring unequal scales or sample sizes | Read axes and context carefully.", 'Distribution comparisons guide educational, medical, manufacturing, and athletic decisions.', 'Two groups have equal medians but IQRs 4 and 15. Which is more consistent? | IQR 4 | Smaller spread means greater consistency. | Compare variability.', 'Create two distributions supporting a nuanced claim: one group has a higher center but also greater variability. Defend the wording.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.SP.B.5 and 7.SP.B.3–4 — Summarize and compare distributions.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP3 and MP6 — Justify and attend to precision."),
    'probability-models-and-simulation' => $build('Probability Models and Simulation', 'Build, test, and revise models of chance using repeated simulation.', 'How can simulation estimate probabilities that are difficult to calculate directly?', "Probability model — outcomes paired with probabilities\nSimulation — repeated chance process representing a real situation\nTrial — one run of an experiment\nRelative frequency — successes divided by trials\nLaw of large numbers — long-run frequencies tend toward probabilities", "Simulate three coin flips with random digits 0–4 as heads and 5–9 as tails. | Record whether at least two heads occur. | Repeat many trials.\nCompare simulated frequency with exact probability 4/8=1/2.", "Using a simulation that does not preserve probabilities | Match each outcome to proportional random-number ranges.\nDrawing a conclusion from too few trials | Increase trials and report variability.", 'Simulation estimates waiting times, risk, genetics, queues, reliability, and complex game outcomes.', 'A fair event has probability 0.30. Assign random digits 0–9. | 0,1,2 as success | Three of ten digits. | Keep outcomes equally likely.', 'Design a valid simulation for a real multistage situation, run enough trials, and compare the estimate with an exact or reasoned benchmark.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.SP.C.6–8 — Approximate and calculate probability.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP4 and MP5 — Model and use tools."),
    'regression-correlation-and-causation' => $build('Regression, Correlation, and Causation', 'Use regression models to describe association while distinguishing correlation from evidence of cause and effect.', 'When does a relationship between variables support prediction, and when can it support a causal claim?', "Regression — method for modeling the relationship between variables\nCorrelation — measure of the direction and strength of a linear association\nCausation — relationship in which changing one variable produces a change in another\nResidual — observed value minus predicted value\nLurking variable — unmeasured factor that may influence both variables", "A regression line for study time x and score y is y=4x+62. | At x=5, the predicted score is 82. | A student scoring 86 has residual 86−82=4.\nIce-cream sales and sunburns rise together | This correlation does not show that ice cream causes sunburn; warm weather affects both.", "Saying correlation proves causation | Consider study design, random assignment, and lurking variables.\nTreating a prediction as an exact result | Regression estimates a typical response and residuals show variation.\nUsing a model far outside the observed domain | Avoid unsupported extrapolation.", 'Regression supports scientific research, education, public health, economics, sports analytics, and business forecasting.', 'A model predicts 48, but the observed value is 53. Find the residual. | 5 | Residual=observed−predicted=53−48. | A positive residual means the point lies above the model.', 'Find or create a context with strong correlation but no reasonable causal link. Identify a lurking variable, explain why the correlation can still support limited prediction, and state what study design would strengthen a causal claim.', "CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.8.SP.A.2–3 — Model linear association and interpret slope and intercept.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.HSS.ID.B.6 and C.9 — Fit functions to data and distinguish correlation from causation.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP3 and MP4 — Construct arguments and model."),
];
